Z={
1:'Jñāneśvar, the thirteenth-century Marathi saint, describes Arjuna here as a thirsty man who has drunk deeply and yet longs for the spring itself. His Jñāneśvarī, a poetic commentary of some nine thousand verses, gives chapter 11 some of its finest passages. Abhinavagupta, the Kashmiri philosopher who commented on the Gītā in the tenth century, reads the whole chapter as a lesson in how vision follows understanding.',
2:'Many temple images of Viṣṇu, such as the reclining Raṅganātha at Śrīraṅgam or Padmanābha at Thiruvananthapuram, are praised in hymns for their lotus eyes. The Ālvārs, the Tamil poet-saints, wrote whole poems on those eyes alone. Arjuna’s epithet here belongs to a very long devotional tradition. Hearing first and seeing later is also the order of the Upaniṣadic method: śravaṇa, then manana and nididhyāsana (Bṛhadāraṇyaka 2.4.5).',
3:'Commentators contrast Arjuna with Duryodhana, who saw a cosmic form in the Kaurava court and dismissed it as a conjuror’s trick. Seeing is not the same as recognising. Arjuna asks with faith, so the vision transforms him. Vedānta Deśika, the great Śrī Vaiṣṇava teacher, notes that the desire to see the Lord is itself a gift of the Lord. Many devotees pray simply for darśan, the sight of God, before any other request.',
4:'Madhusūdana Sarasvatī observes that Arjuna does not doubt the Lord’s power, only his own fitness. That humility is what makes him fit. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad (1.2.23) says the Self reveals itself to the one it chooses. Some teachers compare the moment to a student asking a master musician to play a difficult rāga, knowing that he may not be able to follow it. The answer, in the next verse, is an eager yes.',
5:'Painters in the Rajput, Pahari and South Indian schools have drawn the Viśvarūpa many times. The form is shown filled with gods, sages, animals, oceans and mountains. Each artist chooses differently, because no single image can hold it. Commentators say this verse is an invitation to look at the variety of the world with new eyes. The many colours and shapes are not separate from the One; they are its self-expression.',
6:'The Aśvins, twin horsemen of the dawn, are divine physicians in the Vedas; they are also fathers of Nakula and Sahadeva, two of the Pāṇḍavas. So Arjuna sees within Kṛṣṇa even the fathers of his own brothers. Commentators of the Mādhva school note that all these gods are shown as dependent on Viṣṇu, while Śaṅkara’s followers see them as forms of one Brahman. Both see unity behind the crowd of deities.',
7:'Some readers link this verse to the Chāndogya Upaniṣad’s teaching that the whole universe is present even in the small space of the heart (8.1.3). What Arjuna sees outside, the Upaniṣad says is within. Commentators note the phrase ihaikastham, “here, in one place”: the vision is not of a distant heaven but of this very body standing before him.',
8:'Sañjaya too had been given divine sight by Vyāsa, and so did Dhṛtarāṣṭra, briefly, in the Kaurava court. Commentators note that the gift of sight always comes from grace. Śaṅkara calls it divya cakṣus, a supernatural eye, not ordinary perception. Some modern teachers compare it to the change that comes when we see a familiar person with love: the face is the same, but we see far more. Spiritual sight is a change in the seer as much as in the seen.',
9:'Sañjaya calls Kṛṣṇa mahā-yogeśvara, great lord of yoga, a title that returns in the Gītā’s final verse. Commentators note that yoga here means the Lord’s power to manifest. Abhinavagupta, the Kashmiri commentator, reads the vision as a revelation of consciousness itself, the source of all forms.',
10:'Commentators of the Śrī Vaiṣṇava school, such as Vedānta Deśika, note that the Lord’s weapons are always shown ready, udyata, never at rest. They see this as his constant readiness to protect the devotee. Ornaments, garlands and weapons together show beauty and protection joined in one form.',
11:'In South Indian temples, deities are dressed in silk, garlanded with flowers and anointed with sandal paste and perfumes during festivals. Commentators say the Gītā describes the cosmic form in the same way so that worshippers can recognise their own temple Lord in it. The vision is not foreign to devotion; it is the temple image without limits. Vedānta Deśika’s hymns often move in this way, from the local temple form to the cosmic Lord and back.',
12:'Some saints describe their own visions with the same image of countless suns. The Bengali saint Ramakrishna spoke of an ocean of light. Commentators say the Gītā chooses light because it is the one thing every human knows and cannot look at directly: we see by it, but cannot see it fully.',
13:'Commentators note the word pravibhaktam: the universe is still divided into its many parts as Arjuna sees it. Vedānta Deśika says this shows that the world is real and organised, not chaos. Even in the highest vision, order remains. The cosmos is not dissolved but shown in its true relation to the Lord.',
14:'Indian aesthetics names nine rasas, moods of art; wonder, adbhuta, is one of them. Commentators note that this chapter moves through several: wonder here, then fear (bhayānaka), then peace (śānta) at the end. Arjuna’s goose-flesh and folded hands are classical signs of deep emotion in Sanskrit poetry. The Bhāgavata Purāṇa (11.14.24) lists hair standing on end and a choked voice among the marks of a true devotee.',
15:'Brahmā on the lotus is a familiar image: Viṣṇu lies on the serpent Ananta on the cosmic ocean, a lotus rises from his navel, and Brahmā sits on it to create the world. Arjuna sees the creator himself inside the form. Commentators note the order of beings named: gods, all creatures, Brahmā, the sages, the serpents. Every level of existence is included. The verse begins Arjuna’s long hymn, which many chant on its own.',
16:'The Muṇḍaka Upaniṣad (2.1.4) describes the Supreme with fire as its head and the sun and moon as its eyes. Commentators note Arjuna’s struggle: he looks for edges and finds none. Some modern readers compare the experience to looking at the night sky through a powerful telescope. The more one sees, the further the universe extends. The Gītā’s vision is spiritual rather than astronomical, but the feeling of boundlessness is similar.',
17:'Commentators note durnirīkṣyam, “hard to look at”: even with divine sight, Arjuna struggles. Madhusūdana says the divine eye made the vision possible, not easy. Many mystics speak of the difficulty of bearing great spiritual experience. The Bible tells of Moses, who was told no one could see God’s face and live, and saw only his back (Exodus 33).',
18:'The phrase śāśvata-dharma-goptā links back to Kṛṣṇa’s promise in 4.7–8 to come whenever dharma declines. Arjuna now sees the guardian himself. Commentators note that dharma here means the eternal order that holds the world together, not only rules of conduct. Arjuna’s understanding grows even as his fear grows. He names the Lord with terms from earlier chapters, showing that he has truly listened.',
19:'Commentators note that Arjuna repeats “without beginning, middle or end” from 11.16, as people do when overwhelmed. The fire in the mouth is both the fire of sacrifice and the fire that consumes the worlds. In the Vedic view, the sun, moon and fire are three great lights; here all three belong to the Lord’s face. The verse prepares for the fearsome side of the vision that follows.',
20:'The Katha Upaniṣad (2.3.3) says that from fear of him the fire burns, the sun shines, and Indra, wind and death run their courses. The Gītā’s vision makes that fear visible. Commentators note that the trembling worlds are not punished; they are awed. The Lord’s power holds the universe in order. Many traditions speak of a holy fear, a sense of the sacred so great that it humbles everything else.',
21:'The sages’ blessing “svasti” is still used at the start of Vedic chants, as in the prayer svasti na indro vṛddhaśravāḥ. Commentators note the contrast between the frightened gods and the calm sages: knowledge brings steadiness even before the overwhelming. Śaṅkara says the gods enter the Lord as the destroyer; others praise him. The verse teaches a response to power that is neither panic nor pride.',
22:'Commentators note that both gods and asuras are named together: even enemies of the gods gaze at the Lord in wonder. Śaṅkara says all classes of beings, high and low, are included. The verse shows that before the Supreme, the old wars of gods and demons fall silent.',
23:'Commentators note that Arjuna now admits his own fear: “and so do I”. Madhusūdana praises this honesty as a sign of a mature devotee, who does not pretend. In the Rāmāyaṇa, even Rāma’s brother Lakṣmaṇa and Hanumān admit fear at times. Courage, the epics suggest, is acting rightly despite fear, not never feeling it.',
24:'This is the first time Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa “Viṣṇu” in the Gītā; he will do so again in 11.30. Commentators note that in extremity the true name comes. The Mahābhārata often identifies Kṛṣṇa with Viṣṇu and Arjuna with Nara, his eternal companion. Some teachers say fear can be a doorway: when all our usual supports fall away, we discover what we really trust.',
25:'Commentators note diśo na jāne, “I do not know the directions”: Arjuna has lost his bearings, physically and inwardly. The great archer, whose skill depended on knowing direction and distance, is lost. Some teachers say this disorientation is sometimes necessary before a new orientation, toward the Lord, can be found.',
26:'Commentators note that Arjuna sees the warriors of “our side” too: Abhimanyu, Ghaṭotkaca, Drupada, Virāṭa and others will die. The vision is not a victory parade. Śaṅkara says this shows the Lord’s purpose in the war. The Strī Parva of the Mahābhārata describes the grief of the women of both sides after the war, a moving reminder of its cost.',
27:'Commentators note that heads, uttamāṅga, the “highest limbs”, are crushed: the pride of warriors ends in dust. Śaṅkara says this shows the vanity of the warriors’ pride. The Śānti Parva, after the war, has long reflections by Yudhiṣṭhira on the emptiness of victory won at such cost.',
28:'The rivers-to-the-sea image appears in several Upaniṣads. The Praśna Upaniṣad (6.5) says that as rivers flowing toward the ocean disappear in it, losing name and form, so the sixteen parts of the person disappear in the Supreme. Commentators say the warriors who die bravely in duty are like rivers. They go naturally, without struggle, toward their end.',
29:'Commentators note samṛddha-vegāḥ, “with great speed”: both moths and warriors rush, unable to stop. The Buddhist Dhammapada also warns that craving drives beings like a stream. The Gītā’s point is psychological as well as cosmic: blind desire hurries us toward harm.',
30:'Commentators note the shocking boldness of the image: the Lord licking his lips as he devours. Hindu tradition does not shy away from fierce images of the Divine: Kālī with her tongue out, Bhairava, Narasiṃha. They show that the same power that creates also dissolves. Arjuna, who loves Kṛṣṇa, must also see this face to understand that the war is in larger hands than his.',
31:'Arjuna’s question “who are you?” is one of the most profound in the Gītā. Ramana Maharshi taught the question “who am I?” as a method. Here the question is turned toward God. Commentators note that Arjuna knows the name but not the purpose. The Lord’s answer is the most famous line of the chapter.',
32:'Time, kāla, is personified in Indian thought as Mahākāla, a form of Śiva, and as Yama, lord of death. The Atharva Veda (19.53–54) has hymns to Time as the first cause, carrying all things like a horse with seven reins. Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa identifies with Time here not to frighten but to free. If the outcome is already in Time’s hands, Arjuna can act without the burden of thinking he alone decides who lives and dies.',
33:'Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa also promises glory, yaśas, and a prosperous kingdom: Arjuna will not lose by obeying. Śaṅkara says the Lord gives the worldly reward too, to encourage. Rāmānuja says the glory belongs to the instrument because the Lord shares his honour with his devotees.',
34:'Commentators note that Kṛṣṇa ends with yudhyasva, “fight”, and jetāsi, “you will win”. The command and the promise go together. Madhusūdana says the promise removes Arjuna’s last doubt, the fear of defeat he voiced in 2.6. The war will indeed be won, as Sañjaya’s last verse (18.78) confirms.',
35:'Commentators note that Arjuna bows twice, namaskṛtvā and praṇamya: repeated reverence. Madhusūdana says the trembling is the mixture of fear and love. The verse prepares the hymn of praise that follows, one of the most beautiful in Sanskrit, verses 11.36–46.',
36:'Commentators note that rakṣāṃsi, demonic beings, flee at the Lord’s praise. Many traditions hold that sacred chanting drives away negativity; the Hanumān Cālīsā promises the same. The verse begins with sthāne, “rightly” or “fittingly”: it is fitting that the world rejoices. Praise is not flattery but the right response to what is truly great.',
37:'Commentators note Arjuna calls the Lord jagan-nivāsa, “home of the world”, a second time (also 11.25). The image of the Lord as home is tender: even in the cosmic vision, he is where the world lives. Madhusūdana says the very greatness that frightens is also the reason to bow.',
38:'Commentators note that Arjuna’s titles here echo the Upaniṣads closely. Ādi-deva, the first God, recalls the Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad (6.18), which speaks of the God who created Brahmā and gave him the Vedas. Purāṇa puruṣa, the ancient Person, recalls the Puruṣa Sūkta. A hymn like this is a garland of earlier scripture. Arjuna, a warrior, shows he knows the sacred texts well.',
39:'Prapitāmaha, great-grandsire, is a striking title. Brahmā is called pitāmaha, grandfather of all beings; the Lord, as Brahmā’s source, is one generation further back. Commentators note that this places the Lord before creation itself. Many temples close their worship with a prostration to all directions while reciting this verse.',
40:'Hindu worship often includes pradakṣiṇā, walking around the deity or temple clockwise, and bowing in all directions. Commentators link this practice to Arjuna’s salutations “in front and behind, on every side”. If the Lord is everywhere, every direction is sacred. Some teachers suggest a simple practice: bow inwardly toward each direction, remembering the Divine there.',
41:'Commentators note that the Lord in human form was easy to treat casually precisely because he made himself accessible. Rāmānuja calls this saulabhya, easy accessibility, one of the Lord’s greatest qualities. In the Mahābhārata, Kṛṣṇa says Arjuna is dearer to him than his own life, and that whoever hates Arjuna hates him.',
42:'Commentators note how specific the verse is: playing, resting, sitting, eating, alone or with others. These are the shared moments of a real friendship. Some teachers say that the Lord, being love itself, never kept score. Arjuna’s apology is for himself, so that he can be at peace. It is a model for apologising to those we have taken for granted.',
43:'Commentators note that Arjuna asks a rhetorical question: if none in the three worlds is equal to you, how could any be greater? This echoes the Śvetāśvatara Upaniṣad (6.8), which says no one is found equal to him or greater. The Gītā gathers Upaniṣadic language into Arjuna’s hymn.',
44:'Vaiṣṇava theology describes five relationships with God: peace, service, friendship, parental love and the love of lovers. Three are named in this verse, and later bhakti saints made them central. The Bhāgavata Purāṇa shows Yaśodā’s parental love, the cowherd boys’ friendship and the gopīs’ love for Kṛṣṇa. Commentators say all these forgive easily, and so does the Lord.',
45:'Commentators say Arjuna’s honesty here is complete: he is thrilled and terrified at the same time. Many mystics describe the same mixture. Rudolf Otto called it mysterium tremendum et fascinans, the mystery that makes us tremble and draws us in. Devotional traditions prefer the gentle form for daily love, while honouring the cosmic form as truth.',
46:'Commentators note sahasra-bāho, “thousand-armed one”: Arjuna, still seeing the cosmic form, addresses it while asking for the four-armed one. Madhusūdana says the request shows that the devotee longs for the form he loves, not only for the form that overwhelms.',
47:'Commentators note tejomaya, “made of light”, and ādyam, “primal”: this form is the original source of all forms. Some Vaiṣṇava teachers say the cosmic form is a manifestation of the Lord’s power, while his personal form is his own. Others, following Śaṅkara, see both as appearances of the formless Brahman.',
48:'Commentators note kuru-pravīra, “hero of the Kurus”: Arjuna is reminded that he too belongs to the Kuru line, though on the opposite side from Duryodhana. Madhusūdana says the Lord honours Arjuna by saying no one else has received this gift.',
49:'Commentators note prīta-manāḥ, “glad at heart”: Kṛṣṇa wants Arjuna not only unafraid but happy. Śaṅkara says the gentle form brings joy. Many devotees find this verse comforting after hard experiences: what frightened us need not remain a source of fear.',
50:'Some commentators say Kṛṣṇa first returns to the four-armed form and then to the two-armed human form. The chapter now shifts from triṣṭubh to the gentler anuṣṭubh metre, mirroring the return to the gentle form. Commentators admire this as poetry: the sound of the verses calms as the vision calms.',
51:'Teachers of meditation often say that great experiences must be followed by a return to ordinary life. The test of an experience is how one lives afterward. Arjuna is himself again, but not the same as before. He will now fight with understanding and without the delusion of chapter 1.',
52:'Commentators note sudurdarśam, “very hard to see”: Arjuna’s gift is rare. Madhusūdana says even the gods long for it but do not receive it, because they lack undivided devotion. The verse prepares 11.54, which names that devotion as the only key.',
53:'Commentators note that the list in 11.53 is slightly shorter than in 11.48: Vedas, austerity, gifts, sacrifice. Śaṅkara says the repetition is for emphasis. Madhva says the Lord is never seen by effort alone; grace comes to the devoted.',
54:'Commentators note that 11.54 and 18.55 form a pair: by devotion the Lord is known in truth and entered. The bhakti movements of medieval India, from the Ālvārs to Caitanya, rested on this teaching. Rāmānuja calls ananya bhakti, undivided devotion, the highest means. Śaṅkara agrees that devotion leads to knowledge and union.',
55:'Commentators note that each of the five marks in the verse matches a chapter: work for the Lord (chapter 3), the Lord as goal (chapter 8), devotion (chapter 9), non-attachment (chapter 2), no enmity (chapter 12). The verse thus gathers the Gītā’s teaching so far before chapter 12 turns fully to devotion.',
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
